t.\n\n![](images/c1/c17a5d06bf0ff0b67565880092c6dbdcd085d1460b610f35634f649c88b77998.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761435 1072 #uuid "a27601af-a5b2-5b2c-bf35-e7d92d0686b2" 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761435 1074 "Construct the known component $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=\\frac{4}{3}\\,(3, 0)=(4, 0). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(4, -4).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e6/e6f785f03744709ff1340cc3f6fdad62001df775a1f2972fbd6796844c358875.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(4, -4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{4}{4}=1 \\\\\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{-4}{-4}=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=1$." 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761434 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761436 1071 "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/03/037a78174621485781dff6e86085e2cf8389ff9ae4e148c37346cb23db911b64.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=3\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761436 1072 #uuid "a4ca5188-16aa-55da-a359-95d4c2a72918" 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761436 1074 "Construct the known component $3\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=3\\,(1, -1)=(3, -3). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(-1, 0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/9a/9a193e20dd46765efa19a097c486108c52549134abe3c55757196ed9045bbcd7.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{2}$." 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761437 1080 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761437 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761437 1083 #uuid "a7bc8e7c-9591-57ac-8208-b6dc94487196" 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761438 1080 "1" 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761438 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761438 1083 #uuid "b4cc3525-8080-5721-b0af-2e6ef5ecd2ae" 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761439 1080 "1" 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761439 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547589 17592186576411 true] [17592186761439 1083 #uuid "bccd4032-1759-587a-b3d8-74